('Alebtong District Launches Community-Led Road Maintenance Drive Ahead of Rainy Season', 'alebtong-district-launches-community-led-road-maintenance-drive-ahead-of-rainy-season', 'Alebtong District Local Government has rolled out a labour-based road maintenance programme, mobilising residents to clear culverts and grade feeder roads before the rains set in.', '<p><strong>ALEBTONG DISTRICT</strong> — Local leaders in Alebtong have launched a community-led road maintenance drive covering over 40 kilometres of feeder roads, aiming to beat the onset of the rainy season when many rural routes become impassable.</p><p>The exercise, coordinated by the District Production and Works Office, brings together youth groups, local councillors, and district engineers to clear blocked culverts, cut back overgrown roadside vegetation, and re-grade sections that washed out during the last rains.</p><h2><strong>Labour-Based Approach</strong></h2><p>The District Engineer said the labour-based model was chosen deliberately, both to cut costs and to create short-term income for residents. Participants are paid a daily stipend drawn from the district\'s road fund allocation.</p><p>\"We are targeting the roads that connect farmers to their markets first,\" the Engineer told this publication during a site visit to one of the affected stretches. \"If produce cannot reach the trading centres, the whole local economy slows down.\"</p><h2><strong>Community Reaction</strong></h2><p>Residents along the affected routes welcomed the initiative, though several called for it to be extended to cover more of the district\'s network, which local officials estimate has over 300 kilometres of feeder roads in varying states of disrepair.</p><p>A market vendor from one of the trading centres along the route said transporters had previously been charging extra during the rainy season because of the poor road conditions, a cost that was ultimately passed on to buyers.</p><p>District leadership says the drive will run for the next three weeks, with plans to review progress before the rains fully set in.</p>', NULL, NULL, 2, 3, NULL, NULL, 'draft', 0, 0, '2026-08-01 08:02:08', '2026-08-01 08:02:08', 0, 2, 'inter'),
('Free Medical Camp in Otuke Draws Over 2,000 Residents Seeking Eye and Dental Care', 'free-medical-camp-in-otuke-draws-over-2-000-residents-seeking-eye-and-dental-care', 'A three-day free medical outreach in Otuke District attracted thousands of residents for eye screening, dental checks, and minor surgical procedures, organisers say.', '<p><strong>OTUKE DISTRICT</strong> — A free medical camp held at Otuke Health Centre IV over the weekend drew more than 2,000 residents from across the district, with long queues forming from the first morning for eye and dental services.</p><p>The camp, organised in partnership between the district health office and a coalition of visiting specialists, offered free eye screening, reading glasses, dental extractions, and referrals for cataract surgery, alongside general health checks including blood pressure and diabetes screening.</p><h2><strong>High Turnout</strong></h2><p>The District Health Officer said the turnout exceeded expectations, forcing organisers to extend working hours on the second day to accommodate everyone who had registered.</p><p>\"Many of the people we saw had never had their eyes checked in their lives,\" the Health Officer said. \"Some conditions we identified were advanced simply because there has been no access to this kind of specialised care here before.\"</p><h2><strong>Gaps In Rural Healthcare</strong></h2><p>Local leaders used the event to renew calls for a permanent eye and dental unit at the health centre, noting that residents currently have to travel long distances to Lira City for specialist treatment.</p><p>One elderly resident, who received a pair of reading glasses during the camp, said she had been struggling to thread a needle and read scripture for years before Saturday\'s outreach.</p><p>Organisers say they intend to return to Otuke for a follow-up camp focused on surgical cases identified during this round of screening.</p>', NULL, NULL, 9, 4, NULL, NULL, 'draft', 0, 0, '2026-08-01 02:02:08', '2026-08-01 02:02:08', 0, 2, 'inter'),
('New Science Laboratory Block Commissioned at Kole Secondary School', 'new-science-laboratory-block-commissioned-at-kole-secondary-school', 'Kole Secondary School has commissioned a new science laboratory block funded through a government infrastructure grant, easing years of overcrowding in practical science lessons.', '<p><strong>KOLE DISTRICT</strong> — A newly constructed science laboratory block at Kole Secondary School was officially commissioned this week, giving the school its first dedicated space for physics, chemistry, and biology practicals.</p><p>The facility, funded through a government secondary education infrastructure grant, includes three separate laboratory rooms, a preparation room, and storage for chemicals and equipment — replacing makeshift arrangements that had seen science classes share a single repurposed classroom for over a decade.</p><h2><strong>Long-Standing Need</strong></h2><p>The school\'s head teacher said the lack of proper laboratory space had been one of the biggest obstacles to improving science performance at the school, with students often only seeing practical demonstrations rather than conducting experiments themselves due to space and safety constraints.</p><p>\"For years our science teachers have had to choose which topics to demonstrate practically and which to simply teach in theory, because we did not have the space to do it safely,\" the head teacher said at the commissioning ceremony. \"This block changes that completely.\"</p><h2><strong>District Officials Pledge Support</strong></h2><p>The district education officer, who attended the ceremony, commended the contractor for completing the work within the agreed timeline and urged the school administration to prioritise maintenance of the new facility.</p><p>Parents and local leaders who attended the event called for similar investment in other schools within the sub-county, noting that several nearby secondary schools still lack functional laboratories.</p><p>The school says the new block will be in full use starting next term, with science teachers currently undergoing a short orientation on the new equipment.</p>', NULL, NULL, 10, 1, NULL, NULL, 'draft', 0, 0, '2026-07-31 20:02:08', '2026-07-31 20:02:08', 0, 2, 'inter'),
('Lango Farmers\' Cooperative Secures Market Deal to Export Sunflower Oil Regionally', 'lango-farmers-cooperative-secures-market-deal-to-export-sunflower-oil-regionally', 'A farmers\' cooperative in the Lango sub-region has signed a supply agreement to export processed sunflower oil to buyers in neighbouring regions, a deal leaders say will boost farm-gate prices.', '<p><strong>LIRA DISTRICT</strong> — A cooperative representing sunflower farmers across the Lango sub-region has signed a supply agreement with a regional buyer, securing a market for processed sunflower oil beyond the local trading centres where prices have historically fluctuated.</p><p>The cooperative, which brings together farmer groups from several districts including Lira, Apac, and Dokolo, operates a small processing plant that presses raw sunflower seed into cooking oil for sale.</p><h2><strong>Better Prices For Farmers</strong></h2><p>The cooperative chairperson said the new agreement guarantees a fixed minimum price per kilogram of processed oil for the next planting season, giving member farmers more certainty when planning their harvests.</p><p>\"In past seasons, some of our farmers were forced to sell raw seed cheaply to middlemen because we had nowhere else to take it,\" the chairperson said. \"This agreement means we can process more of it ourselves and sell at a fairer price.\"</p><h2><strong>Expansion Plans</strong></h2><p>Cooperative leaders say they plan to use proceeds from the deal to expand processing capacity at the plant, including the purchase of an additional oil press to handle higher volumes during peak harvest.</p><p>Agricultural extension workers in the area have encouraged more farmers to take up sunflower growing as a cash crop, citing its relatively low input costs compared to other cash crops grown in the region.</p><p>The cooperative expects the first shipment under the new agreement to go out before the end of the current quarter.</p>', NULL, NULL, 4, 3, NULL, NULL, 'draft', 0, 0, '2026-07-31 14:02:08', '2026-07-31 14:02:08', 0, 2, 'inter'),
('Lira Town Council Hosts Inaugural Lango Sub-Region Youth Football Tournament', 'lira-town-council-hosts-inaugural-lango-sub-region-youth-football-tournament', 'Sixteen teams from across the Lango sub-region are competing in a new youth football tournament hosted in Lira, organisers say, with hopes of scouting talent for regional academies.', '<p><strong>LIRA CITY</strong> — Sixteen youth football teams drawn from districts across the Lango sub-region kicked off the inaugural Lango Youth Football Tournament at Barlonyo Playgrounds over the weekend, in what organisers describe as the region\'s largest grassroots football event in recent years.</p><p>The tournament, open to players under the age of 18, features teams from Lira, Apac, Oyam, Dokolo, Alebtong, and Kole districts, competing in a group-stage format over two weeks before knockout rounds determine the eventual champions.</p><h2><strong>Talent Scouting</strong></h2><p>Organisers say the tournament was designed partly as a scouting platform, with representatives from regional football academies attending matches to identify promising young players.</p><p>\"We have a lot of raw talent in this region that never gets seen because there are so few organised competitions at this level,\" the tournament\'s lead organiser said. \"Our goal is for this to become an annual fixture on the football calendar.\"</p><h2><strong>Community Turnout</strong></h2><p>Opening day matches drew large crowds of spectators, with local vendors setting up stalls around the playgrounds to sell refreshments to fans.</p><p>Several team coaches said the tournament had given their players a level of exposure and competitive experience they had not previously had access to, particularly players from more rural sub-counties.</p><p>The final is scheduled to take place in two weeks, with local leaders expected to attend and present trophies to the winning team.</p>', NULL, NULL, 5, 4, NULL, NULL, 'draft', 0, 0, '2026-07-31 08:02:08', '2026-07-31 08:02:08', 0, 2, 'inter'),
('Traditional Lango Cultural Gala Returns to Boroboro Grounds After Three-Year Hiatus', 'traditional-lango-cultural-gala-returns-to-boroboro-grounds-after-three-year-hiatus', 'The annual Lango cultural gala returned to Boroboro Grounds this weekend after a three-year break, featuring traditional dance, music, and displays of Lango heritage.', '<p><strong>LIRA DISTRICT</strong> — Thousands of residents turned out at Boroboro Grounds over the weekend for the return of the annual Lango cultural gala, a celebration of traditional dance, music, and craft that had not been held in three years.</p><p>The event featured performances from cultural troupes across the sub-region, showcasing traditional Lango dances such as <em>Otole</em> and <em>Larakaraka</em>, alongside displays of traditional attire, food, and handmade crafts from local artisans.</p><h2><strong>Preserving Heritage</strong></h2><p>Organisers from the Lango Cultural Foundation said the gala\'s return was a deliberate effort to keep younger generations connected to their heritage, noting a decline in participation in traditional practices in recent years.</p><p>\"Many of our young people can recite the lyrics to songs in other languages but do not know the songs their grandparents sang,\" one of the event\'s organisers said. \"Events like this are how we keep that from disappearing entirely.\"</p><h2><strong>Cultural Leaders Attend</strong></h2><p>Several cultural and clan leaders from across the sub-region attended the gala, using the platform to speak on the importance of passing down oral history, naming customs, and traditional governance structures to younger community members.</p><p>Vendors selling traditional foods and crafts reported brisk business throughout the day, with some travelling from neighbouring districts specifically to attend the event.</p><p>Organisers say they intend to make the gala an annual fixture going forward, with plans already underway for next year\'s edition.</p>', NULL, NULL, 6, 1, NULL, NULL, 'draft', 0, 0, '2026-07-31 02:02:08', '2026-07-31 02:02:08', 0, 2, 'inter');
